  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $y=f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities.",
